---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
const baseUrl = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.endsWith("/") ? import.meta.env.BASE_URL : `${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}/`;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Planning &amp; Building Permits - NV Studio Design"
  description="Zoning review, feasibility studies, and residential permit drawings for GTA and Ontario municipalities. Additions, new homes, secondary suites, and laneway houses."
  keywords="building permit drawings Toronto, zoning review GTA, residential permits Ontario, laneway house permits Toronto, committee of adjustment Toronto"
>
  <section id="one">
    <div class="inner">
      <header class="major">
        <h1>Planning &amp; Building Permits</h1>
      </header>

      <p>
        Permits are where a good idea meets a municipal reviewer. This page
        is for homeowners and builders who want to understand what that
        actually involves in the GTA before they start.
      </p>

      <p>
        A short version: permits are <strong>part of the design work</strong>,
        not a separate product. Every project we take on is designed with
        the Zoning By-law, the Ontario Building Code, and the reviewing
        municipality in mind from the first sketch. When we talk about
        &ldquo;permit drawings,&rdquo; we mean the specific set of drawings
        the city needs to issue the permit &mdash; prepared from the same
        design, not redrawn.
      </p>

      <h2>Zoning &amp; feasibility</h2>
      <p>
        Before anyone draws the house, we check what the lot allows. The
        Zoning By-law governs things like setbacks, building height, lot
        coverage, floor space index (FSI), and how much of the lot can be
        hard-surfaced. Overlays &mdash; heritage districts, ravine
        protection, tree protection, and similar &mdash; add more rules on
        top.
      </p>
      <p>
        A feasibility review answers the practical question: &ldquo;Can the
        project you have in mind actually be built on this lot?&rdquo; If
        the answer is &ldquo;not as-is,&rdquo; we identify what would need
        to change &mdash; a smaller footprint, a minor variance, or a
        different programme altogether.
      </p>

      <h2>Minor variances &amp; Committee of Adjustment</h2>
      <p>
        Sometimes the by-law forbids something small but essential &mdash; a
        dormer a little too close to a side yard, a back addition a few
        feet past the setback. In those cases we prepare the package for
        Committee of Adjustment, attend the hearing, and work with
        neighbours and planning staff to land the approval.
      </p>

      <h2>Permit drawings</h2>
      <p>
        A residential permit set typically includes site plan, floor plans,
        elevations, sections, structural and mechanical coordination
        drawings, and the schedules and notes each municipality expects.
        We draw to what the reviewing office actually asks for &mdash; the
        requirements differ between Toronto, Mississauga, Vaughan,
        Oakville, and the smaller Ontario municipalities &mdash; and we
        respond to the examiner&rsquo;s comments until the permit issues.
      </p>

      <h2>When this service is relevant</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>You&rsquo;re planning a new custom home and need a complete permit submission.</li>
        <li>You&rsquo;re adding a second storey, rear addition, or dormer to an existing house.</li>
        <li>You&rsquo;re adding a secondary suite, basement apartment, or laneway house.</li>
        <li>You&rsquo;re renovating and the scope touches structure, egress, plumbing, or the building envelope.</li>
        <li>You&rsquo;ve been asked by your city for a minor variance and don&rsquo;t know where to start.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>When it isn&rsquo;t</h2>
      <p>
        Purely cosmetic interior work &mdash; paint, finishes, non-load-bearing
        updates &mdash; usually doesn&rsquo;t need a permit. If you&rsquo;re
        not sure whether your project does, ask us or your municipality
        before committing to a schedule.
      </p>

      <h2>How it fits with the rest of the work</h2>
      <p>
        If you&rsquo;re already engaging us for
        <a href={`${baseUrl}architectural-design`}>architectural design</a>,
        zoning and permits are folded into that process &mdash; they
        don&rsquo;t need to be commissioned separately. If you just need
        drawings for an existing design or a technical project, we take
        that on too.
      </p>

      <ul class="actions">
        <li><a href={`${baseUrl}contact`} class="button primary">Book a Consultation</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
